+
+# OSEP-0025: Foil Runtime (Copy-on-Write Folder Sandboxes)
+
+<!-- toc -->
+- [Summary](#summary)
+- [Motivation](#motivation)
+  - [Goals](#goals)
+  - [Non-Goals](#non-goals)
+- [Requirements](#requirements)
+- [Proposal](#proposal)
+  - [Notes/Constraints/Caveats](#notesconstraintscaveats)
+  - [Risks and Mitigations](#risks-and-mitigations)
+- [Design Details](#design-details)
+- [Test Plan](#test-plan)
+- [Drawbacks](#drawbacks)
+- [Alternatives](#alternatives)
+- [Infrastructure Needed](#infrastructure-needed)
+- [Upgrade & Migration Strategy](#upgrade--migration-strategy)
+<!-- /toc -->
+
+## Summary
+
+Add a third runtime, `runtime.type = "foil"`, whose sandboxes are copy-on-write
+branches of a folder on the host, made by [foil](https://github.com/umbarsariya/foil)
+(MIT OR Apache-2.0). A sandbox starts in about 25 ms on Linux without Docker,
+runs on Linux and macOS laptops, and what it changed can be reviewed and
+applied to the real folder. Every OpenSandbox client (SDKs, CLI, MCP) works
+unchanged: `foil serve` implements the lifecycle API and an execd-compatible
+API inside each sandbox, and the new `FoilSandboxService` forwards to it.
+
+## Motivation
+
+The most common sandbox job for coding agents is "work on this repository".
+Today that means building or pulling an image, copying the repository into a
+container, and copying results back out. Locally it needs Docker; on macOS it
+needs a Linux VM. Nothing tells the caller which files the agent changed, and
+nothing checks, when results go back, whether someone changed the same files
+meanwhile.
+
+OSEP-0013 brings copy-on-write *inside* a sandbox (bwrap overlay, `diff`,
+`commit` with `overwrite`). A foil runtime makes the sandbox itself the
+copy-on-write layer, over the real folder, and adds what OSEP-0013 lists as
+v2: conflict-refusing commit, per-sandbox cgroup limits, and crash-safe
+application of the changes.
+
+### Goals
+
+- `runtime.type = "foil"`: create, get, list, delete, pause, resume, renew,
+  patch metadata and get endpoints, through the existing lifecycle API.
+- Unmodified SDKs: `Sandbox.create(...)`, `commands.run`, sessions,
+  background commands, files and metrics work as with Docker.
+- Sandbox start, until execd answers, without an image pull. Measured
+  (median of 10, `integrations/opensandbox/bench.py` in foil): Linux 25 ms
+  (35 ms with `--strict`) for a folder of 10,000 files, independent of its
+  size; macOS (APFS clones, so it grows with the file count) 30 ms for a
+  small folder, 0.8 s for 10,000 files. A first command takes 10-18 ms.
+- Review and apply: the changes of a sandbox as a list or a patch, and a
+  commit to the real folder that refuses files changed there since the
+  sandbox started (unless forced).
+- Isolation per sandbox from foil's `--strict` mode where the operator asks
+  for it: deny-by-default filesystem view, PID/IPC namespaces, no network but
+  an allowlist proxy (`networkPolicy` with `defaultAction: deny` maps to it).
+
+### Non-Goals
+
+- Images. A foil sandbox is a view of a host folder; `image.uri` is recorded,
+  not run. Use Docker or Kubernetes for image-defined environments.
+- A security boundary for hostile code on a shared host. Foil shares the
+  host kernel (namespaces on Linux, Seatbelt on macOS). For hostile code run
+  foil in a microVM, or use the existing secure runtimes.
+- Snapshots (OSEP-0008/0015); `snapshotId` and `volumes` are refused.
+
+## Requirements
+
+- No change to the lifecycle or execd specs; no change to SDKs.
+- The runtime is optional: no new dependency for Docker/Kubernetes users.
+- Unsupported requests fail loudly (400), never silently: `volumes`,
+  `snapshotId`, `credentialProxy`, and network policies foil can't enforce
+  (CIDR targets, deny rules under allow-by-default).
+- Credentials never enter a sandbox: the server's key and each sandbox's
+  execd token are checked by `foil serve`, outside the sandbox.
+
+## Proposal
+
+```text
+SDK / CLI / MCP
+   │  lifecycle API                     execd API (direct, or via server proxy)
+   ▼                                        │
+OpenSandbox server ── FoilSandboxService ── ▼ ─────────────┐
+                        │ HTTP (same lifecycle API)         │
+                        ▼                                   ▼
+                  foil serve  ── /v1/sandboxes/{id}/proxy/44772/... ──► Unix socket
+                        │                                            ┌──────────────┐
+                        └─ foil run -b osb-<id> [--strict ...] ──────► foil __execd  │
+                           (one long-lived run per sandbox)          │ commands,    │
+                                                                     │ files, ...   │
+                                                                     └──────────────┘
+                                                                    branch of the folder
+```
+
+- `foil serve -w DIR [foil run options]` serves the lifecycle API. Each
+  sandbox is a branch of `DIR` with one long-lived `foil run` whose command
+  is foil's execd: everything execd starts is inside the branch and the
+  sandbox. `foil serve` relays execd's API from
+  `/v1/sandboxes/{id}/proxy/44772/` to the daemon's Unix socket.
+- `FoilSandboxService` (in this repository, ~250 lines) forwards each
+  `SandboxService` call to `foil serve` and converts the JSON to the
+  server's models. `get_endpoint` returns `foil serve`'s relay address and
+  the sandbox's `X-EXECD-ACCESS-TOKEN` header; the server's own proxy mode
+  works as for other runtimes.
+- Review and apply are `foil serve` endpoints (`GET .../diff`,
+  `POST .../commit`); the service exposes them as `get_diff` / `commit`.
+  A follow-up could add them to the lifecycle spec for every runtime that
+  can support them.
+
+### Notes/Constraints/Caveats
+
+- Paths: on Linux a sandbox sees the folder at its real path; on macOS a
+  branch is a folder of its own, and foil's execd maps the folder's path to
+  it in file APIs (commands see the branch path).
+- `resourceLimits` from the request are not applied; the operator sets
+  limits for all sandboxes with `foil serve --memory/--cpus/--pids`.
+- `uid`/`gid` other than the server user's are refused (foil is unprivileged).
+- Sandboxes outlive `foil serve` restarts: runs are recorded and adopted.
+
+### Risks and Mitigations
+
+| Risk | Mitigation |
+|------|------------|
+| Shared kernel | Documented non-goal; `--strict` by default for untrusted agents; microVM for hostile code |
+| A sandbox reaching another sandbox's execd | Per-sandbox socket folder; a strict view shows only its own; tested |
+| Server key leaking into sandboxes | Removed from the environment of every run; tested |
+| Commit clobbering concurrent edits | Commit refuses files changed since the branch was made (ctime), unless forced; crash-safe journal |
+
+## Design Details
+
+- Config: `[foil] url, api_key | api_key_env, workspace, endpoint_host,
+  timeout_seconds`; `runtime.type` gains `"foil"`. The snapshot runtime for
+  `foil` is the no-op runtime.
+- Errors from `foil serve` keep their HTTP status, with codes prefixed
+  `FOIL::`; an unreachable `foil serve` is a 503.
+- Diagnostics: logs scope `run` (the sandbox's foil run output), events
+  scope `runtime` (state transitions).
+- execd compatibility in foil: `/ping`, `/command` (foreground SSE,
+  background with status, byte-cursor logs, interrupt), `/session`,
+  `/files/*`, `/directories/*`, `/metrics`. `/code`, `/pty` and
+  `/v1/isolated/*` answer 501 / `available: false`.
+
+## Test Plan
+
+- `server/tests/test_foil_service.py`: the service against a mocked
+  `foil serve` (request mapping, errors, endpoints), and against a real one
+  when `FOIL_BIN` is set (create, command, diff, commit).
+- foil's CI runs the unmodified Python SDK end to end against `foil serve`
+  (Linux and macOS, plain and `--strict`), and this server's foil tests.
+- Manually verified: the SDK through this server with `runtime.type =
+  "foil"`, direct and `use_server_proxy`.
+
+## Drawbacks
+
+- A runtime whose sandboxes don't come from images may surprise users who
+  expect `image` to matter; the runtime records it and documents this.
+- An external binary (`foil`) to install and keep running next to the server.
+
+## Alternatives
+
+- foil inside Docker sandboxes (works today with
+  `bootstrap.execd.isolation=enable`; see foil's
+  `integrations/opensandbox/inside`): many attempts in one sandbox, but still
+  a container per sandbox.
+- Client-side only (`foil serve` without this server): already possible,
+  but loses the server's auth, tenancy and tooling.
+
+## Infrastructure Needed
+
+None. CI needs the foil binary for the real-server test (downloadable
+release, or skipped).
+
+## Upgrade & Migration Strategy
+
+Additive: a new runtime type and config block; existing configurations are
+unaffected.
